@meeting promo.ceremony
// Pillar 5 Step 3, the officer ceremony (Day 11, Thursday, 17:15): the observation room on Deck 1, the conference
// table and the stern windows. The Captain, Commander Vance, Calder, Torres, and up to three friends (walk-ons, no
// lines). The Captain reads the commission and pins the insignia ("pins": the colour change), a handshake, one line from
// you, a toast with the Captain's tea. Her Mail on Day 10 asked you to bring an answer: what should an officer never
// forget? Seat C2 plays it: the line marked {pin} bakes pin: true (the insignia goes on; C2's onPick), and the one
// choice comes after it. Head writer W2.
title: Your commission
from: Captain Odile Marrow
gist: The Captain commissioned you as an Officer of the Wren. The framed commission is on your cabin shelf.
== lines
vance: Attention, please. The Captain.   [nod]
marrow: Thank you, Teo. Sit, everyone, if there are chairs. There never are.   [laugh]
marrow > you: Cadet. Chief Calder tells me you're ready. The Chief doesn't say that often.
marrow: By the Survey Service, I commission you an Officer of the Wren. With all that comes with it.
marrow: Hold still. Pins are not my strong point.   [gesture] {pin}
marrow > you: There. Now, my question. What should an officer never forget?
? choice
* The people. Everyone aboard.   rel:marrow+2 mem:promo.answer.people
  marrow: Good answer. The ship is the people in it. The rest is metal.   [nod]
* To ask when I don't know.   rel:marrow+2 mem:promo.answer.ask
  marrow: Good answer. The ones who stop asking are the ones who worry me.   [nod]
* To mind the step in the ready room.   rel:marrow+1 mem:promo.answer.step
  marrow: Ha. Calder warned me about you. Also correct.   [laugh]
calder: Well done.   [nod]
torres-zeke: Told everyone. Didn't I tell everyone?   [laugh]
marrow: Tea, then. Mine's the good stuff. Don't tell the galley.
marrow > you: Welcome, Officer. My door is open. Mind the step.
